* sedit file source vs non-file source
** clal-sedit API extension                                      [DONE 1.9.111]

First point, currently CLAL-SEDIT takes only a string path to edit a whole file.

We cannot tell it: edit a file starting with a selection containing
this function or this toplevel form.

But in the debugger, we know we are debugging a specific function or toplevel form.
So we need to extend the CLAL-SEDIT API to be able to specify it.


=(clal-sedit object)=

~object~: optional. Resolves as follows:
- omitted or ~nil~ -> a stand-alone form starting at ~nil~;
- a symbol -> recall its  recorded definition (by name) (no relation with the source file);
- a string -> a file or directory path;
- any other value -> edited as a sexp.

Let's admit a variadic API:

=(clal-sedit [type] object)=


If the type is omited, the legacy behavior is implemented. With the
type, we do as specified in the following table: 


| type      | object                             | sedit action                                                  |
|-----------+------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------|
| NIL       | any-printable-lisp-object          | the object is the initial selection                           |
| OBJECT    | any-printable-lisp-object          | the object is the initial selection                           |
|           |                                    |                                                               |
| FUNCTION  | funcname:symbol                    | if the function has a source file,                            |
|           |                                    | then edit the function in the context of the file             |
|           |                                    | ie. equivalent to (clal-sedit 'file '("source.lsp" funcname)) |
|           |                                    | else edit the function lambda expression                      |
|           |                                    | ie. equivalent to (clal-sedit 'object lambda-expression)      |
|           |                                    |                                                               |
| FUNCTION  | (file-path:string funcname:symbol) | edit the function funcname in the file at path                |
| FILE      | (file-path:string funcname:symbol) | (ie. edit the file, selecting the function in it).            |
|           |                                    | If the file or the function don't exit, an error              |
|           |                                    | is signaled.                                                  |
|           |                                    |                                                               |
| FILE      | (file:source-position form:sexp)   | edit the form stored in the file and position specified by    |
|           |                                    | the file source-position.                                     |
|           |                                    |                                                               |
| FILE      | (file-path:string linum:integer)   | edit the comment, or the form at line linum (or just after    |
|           |                                    | line linum).                                                  |
|           |                                    |                                                               |
| FILE      | file-path:string                   | edit the file (first form or comment in                       |
|           |                                    | the file is selected)                                         |
|           |                                    | if the file doesn't exist, a warning about it                 |
|           |                                    | is issued, and it's created on save.                          |
|           |                                    |                                                               |
| DIRECTORY | directory-path:string              | edit the directory                                            |
| FILE      | directory-path:string              | edit the directory                                            |
|           |                                    |                                                               |


** Here things must be discriminated.

The debugger (and in general), may ask clal-sedit to edit forms that
come from various sources, some backed by files, and some from memory.

A form may come from different sources:

- read from a source file,

- read from a compiled file, referencing a source file that is available.
  The form may be retrieved from the compiled file,
  or read again from the source file.

- a compiled file, referencing a source file that isn't available.
  The form may be retrieved from the compiled file.
  
- the lisp REPL (edited and entered on the REPL, evaluated by the lisp
  REPL).

- freshly edited in sedit and evaled from sedit (e command)

- freshly edited in sedit and evaled by (EVAL (clal-sedit ...))

- generated by program and evaled by EVAL

- furthermore, we may have forms that have been loaded from files
  originally, but that have been edited with sedit, sedit having saved
  them back to the file (clean), or not (dirty). In both case, the
  source of the edited form may be lost or mangled (clal-sedit reads
  subforms from the terminal, and may reuse subforms from different
  files with copy-and-paste) in the new form.

A form that has source file information can be edited by clal-sedit with
the new parameters specifying the source file:

#+begin_src lisp
  (clal-sedit 'file (list (source-position-file (position-of toplevel-form))
                          (source-position-start-line (position-of toplevel-form))))
  ;; or:
  (clal-sedit 'file (list (position-of toplevel-form) toplevel-form))
#+end_src

If clal-sedit uses the following API to save the source-file while
maintaining the source-position-table, we won't have the last case
where editing forms from files with sedit give us forms that are not
associated with source-position anymore. The following API ensure that
new forms get their own source-position, and old forms
source-positions are updated.

Furthermore, the following API would allow sedit to write forms that
have been edited or entered independently from file, to new files, and
thus having their source-position set for further file editing. This
would require a specific sedit command that will be specified in a
separate issue.

The debugger can base its decision to call clal-sedit with the file
editing option only on the presence of source-position information on
the considered form.

*** source file and position editor

When we edit a function with sedit, and save it to a file, and
evaluate it to integrate it into the lisp image, we don't want to
reload the whole file just to record the file position with the new
form.

If we let sedit save an edited file (new forms, deleted forms,
modified forms), then all the other positions of forms in the file
from the first modification become invalid in the
=*source-position-table*=.

Therefore we will not let sedit edit files that have been loaded (or
loaded and compiled, assuming the compiler transports the
source-position data from the source).

We need a source-file editing module synchronized with the
*source-position-table* and with high level editing functions that
maintain the consistency of the *source-position-table*.


The API to read source files could be used by READ (so that
source-file positions are updated). It would just have to drop
comments, and unwrap atoms. On the other hand an editor like sedit
would keep othe comments and atom (all toplevel items) for editing.

#+begin_src lisp

  (source-file-open path read-or-update) -> source
  ;; open the source file at path for reading or for editing (updating).
  ;; read-or-update is 'READ or 'UPDATE

  ;; read operation:

  (source-file-read source eof-indicator) -> comment-line or comment-block or toplevel-atom or toplevel-form or EOF indicator.
  ;; comment-line and comment-block are structure with source-position and the list of strings of comment lines.
  ;; toplevel-atom is a structure with the source-position and the toplevel atom.
  ;; toplevel-form is a cons. The source-position of the toplevel-form is recorded or updated in the source-position-table.

  (source-file-close source)
  ;; close a source file.


  ;; update operations:

  (source-file-insert-toplevel-form  source ilinenum form)
  (source-file-replace-toplevel-form source flinenum form)
  (source-file-delete-toplevel-form  source flinenum)

  ;; Inserts, replaces or deletes a toplevel form.
  ;;
  ;; The toplevel form replaced or deleted is identified by flinenum
  ;; which should be the first line of the toplevel form.
  ;;
  ;; If flinenum or ilinenum fall in the middle of a form it's an error.
  ;;
  ;; The form is formatted and indented and written to the file from
  ;; ilinenum or flinenum position. This could include 2 white lines
  ;; following the form.
  ;;
  ;; The source-file position information of the form is updated
  ;; (unless it's an atom).


  (source-file-insert-comment source linenum new-comment-lines)
  (source-file-replace-comment source start-linum end-linum new-comment-lines)
  (source-file-delete-comment source start-linum end-linum)

  ;; Inserts, replaces or deletes a toplevel comment.
  ;;
  ;; If linenum, start-linum or end-linum fall in the middle of a
  ;; form, it's an error.
  ;;
  ;; If the interval start-linum (inclusive) end-linum (exclusive)
  ;; contain other than comment or empty (blank) lines, it's an error.
  ;;
  ;; new-comment-lines is a list of strings one perline.
  ;; The whole forms a comment: it may be single-line comments
  ;; prefixed by ; ;; ;;; or ;;;;
  ;; or it may be a comment block with ";|" as first line and "|;" as last line.


  ;; All the source-file operations maintain the
  ;; source-position-table, incrementing or decrementing the position
  ;; that follow the edited lines.

  (source-file-save-and-close source)
  ;; write all the modifications to the file and close it.

#+end_src

Let sedit use this module to save its edits to the file, the
source-position-table and the new forms may be maintained with
up-to-date information.


